Each class in the OSI model should encapsulate the specific roles and responsibilities associated with its layer. I'll detail the interfaces and methods needed for each class, aiming for clarity and to cover the most common functionalities for each layer.

### Physical Layer (Layer 1)
The physical layer deals with the physical transmission of bits over a communications channel.

**Interface:**
```cpp
class PhysicalLayer {
public:
    PhysicalLayer();
    virtual ~PhysicalLayer();

    // Allows creation of the physical network interface
    void createPhysicalLink(const std::string& interfaceName);

    // Start/Stop the physical networking interface
    void start();
    void stop();

    // Start/Stop the transmit/receive operations
    void startTransmit();
    void stopTransmit();

    // Send a bit stream (filled with 1s and 0s)
    void sendBitStream(const std::vector<bool>& bitStream);

    // Receive a bit stream
    std::vector<bool> receiveBitStream();

private:
    bool isTransmitting;
    bool isOperational;
};
```

### Data Link Layer (Layer 2)
The data link layer deals with the transmission of data units, such as frames or packets, over a physical communication channel.

**Interface:**
```cpp
class DataLinkLayer {
public:
    DataLinkLayer();
    virtual ~DataLinkLayer();

    // Send a frame over the network
    void sendFrame(const std::vector<uint8_t>& frame);

    // Receive a frame from the network
    std::vector<uint8_t> receiveFrame();

    // Handle packet arrival or departure
    void handlePacketArrival(const std::vector<uint8_t>& packet);
    void handlePacketDeparture(const std::vector<uint8_t>& packet);

private:
    bool isOperational;
    std::vector<uint8_t> nextGoBit;
};
```

### Network Layer (Layer 3)
The network layer deals with routing data units between different nodes.

**Interface:**
```cpp
class NetworkLayer {
public:
    NetworkLayer();
    virtual ~NetworkLayer();

    // Send a packet to a target IP address
    void sendPacket(const std::string& destinationAddress, const std::vector<uint8_t>& data);

    // Receive a packet from the network
    void receivePacket();

private:
    bool isOperational;
    std::vector<uint8_t> nextGoBit;
};
```

### Transport Layer (Layer 4)
The transport layer deals with the transport of data along specific communication paths.

**Interface:**
```cpp
class TransportLayer {
public:
    TransportLayer();
    virtual ~TransportLayer();

    // Send a transport segment
    void sendSegment(const uint16_t destinationPort, const std::vector<uint8_t>& data);

    // Receive a transport segment
    std::vector<uint8_t> receiveSegment();

private:
    bool isOperational;
    std::vector<uint8_t> nextGoBit;
};
```

### Session Layer (Layer 5)
The session layer provides a virtual connection between applications, allowing them to maintain conversations or sessions.

**Interface:**
```cpp
class SessionLayer {
public:
    SessionLayer();
    virtual ~SessionLayer();

    // Start/End a protocol session
    void beginSession();
    void endSession();

    // Send/receive messages to/from a peer
    void sendMessage(const std::string& message);
    std::string receiveMessage();

private:
    bool isOperational;
    std::vector<uint8_t> nextGoBit;
};
```

### Presentation Layer (Layer 6)
The presentation layer translates data into a form suitable for applications.

**Interface:**
```cpp
class PresentationLayer {
public:
    PresentationLayer();
    virtual ~PresentationLayer();

    // Encode data into the appropriate format
    std::string encodeData(const std::string& data);
    // Decode data back to its original form
    std::string decodeData(const std::string& encodedData);

private:
    bool isOperational;
    std::vector<uint8_t> nextGoBit;
};
```

### Application Layer (Layer 7)
The application layer provides the interface for end users or system software.

**Interface:**
```cpp
class ApplicationLayer {
public:
    ApplicationLayer(const std::string& appName);
    virtual ~ApplicationLayer();

    // Request services from the application
    void requestService(const std::string& request);
    // Handle responses from the application
    std::string handleResponse();

private:
    std::string appName;
    bool isOperational;
    std::vector<uint8_t> nextGoBit;
};
```

### Exposing and Hiding Methods
- **Public Methods:** These interface methods should be public so that objects in different layers can use them to interact.
- **Private Methods:** The internal workings, such as handling bit streams, packet bits, and other low-level details, should be encapsulated in private methods.

